he u.s. that covers the use  disclosure and security of  tected health information and protected  health information is a specific defined  category in HIPAA but essentially it's  it's patient information information  relating to the care of the patient any  bills they receive etc for HIPAA  specifically it applies to two types of  organizations in the u.s. covered  entities and business associates and  these categories just define how  healthcare organizations operate covered  entities are generally the point of care  the insurance companies the healthcare  data clearing houses and then the  business associates are any vendors or  partners of the covered entity that may  do data processing storage or  transmission on the on behalf of the  covered entity again as mentioned  there's no official certification for  HIPAA and the requirements are not very  prescriptive however they they do give a  general overview of how data should be  used collected protected stores and so  on there's another example of this one  you know in the EU that covers the  entire EU jurisdiction and covers some  foreign jurisdiction where EU citizen  data may be processed or it or  transmitted so in GDP are again you'll  see there's a parallel to HIPAA and that  it covers the scope processing and  transfer of personal data in this case  personal data includes some aspects of  healthcare data as well the GD P R does  not use the same language as HIPAA but  it says u GD P R is applicable to data  controllers and data processors there  again there is an exact parallel to  covered entities and business associates  under HIPAA but you can tell that  they're trying to segment the categories  of people who have collected and have  ownership of the data versus people who  have temporary access for a specific  case and finally again there's no  certification for GD P R and  organizations have to kind of align with  its guidance going into the next topic  the next topic is government frameworks  you can think of these as requirements  for contracting with government agencies  the penalties here are not  usually as severe as for the data  privacy regulation however they can  still be very material breach of  government frameworks can jeopardize  contracts with government agencies or it  can result in lawsuits to recoup any  damages here the certification  requirements are generally a bit more  prescriptive we'll go into a couple  examples later but unlike the data  privacy regulation which contained  general guidance government frameworks  tend to be very specific they can have  hundreds or thousands of specific  controls that an organization has to  implement test and document and there  are different degrees of compliance  depending on the framework so one common  example here again for a us-centric  view of things is called FedRAMP and  fred ramp is a US government requirement  for cloud service providers specifically  in order to allow the government agency  to purchase cloud provider solutions and  FedRAMP tends to be very prescriptive  and there are multiple levels of  compliance so organizations can meet you  know a number of different bars of  FedRAMP compliance which allows them to  operate certain workloads or sells  certain levels of technology to the US  government agencies there are you know a  lot of government frameworks depending  on where in the world you are which  government agencies you're working with  another example that comes up is the the  UK National Health Service toolkits and  these are you know guidelines for  technology outsourcing they're a little  bit less prescriptive than FedRAMP but  still more prescriptive than data  privacy regulation there is no official  certification here so you will see that  government frameworks tend to be a  little bit more varied on whether they  have an official certification path or  not another category of requirements  comes from industry standards and  industry standards tend to be committee  driven for a specific market or use case  most require an independent third party  audit it's not something that you can  gender  they self attest to within an  organization there has to be some sort  of checks and balances in place and in  you know most importantly participation  and industry standards is voluntary in  general but it's often enforced on a  contractual basis between a vendor and  their customer so there's no specific  penalties like there are for data  privacy regulation that you'd get from  the government specifically but you may  be susceptible to specific agreements  with your vendors or your customers  depending on what industry standards are  in scope for that relationship there are  a few examples here and most people are  generally familiar with the sock family  of standards and these are globally  recognized standards for IT operations  security availability confidentially  confidentiality integrity and so on this  is you know a very general purpose  standard that's that's useful for a lot  of technology providers across different  domains and then we can look at two  specific industry standard examples one  for healthcare and one for financial  services obviously these aren't mutually  exclusive there are financial services  organizations that operate in the  healthcare space so you know it's  important to understand that the domain  of the industry standard does not  necessarily map one to one with the  organizations that have to comply with  it as part of their business obligations  and the last thing to touch upon in this  session is good practice regulations and  good practice or GXP regulations are  actually a combination of government  regulation industry standard tribal  knowledge and other organizations  specific controls they tend to be more  nebulous for specific use cases however  some examples that are relevant to the  healthcare and life sciences industry  are good clinical practice good  laboratory practice and good  manufacturing 