ecommendations around Identity and  Access Management endpoint security how  we allow external users to connect to  your systems how you record accesses by  external users and make sure there's a  permanent audit trail and so on and so  forth so at this point it's important to  kind of transition to see ok how do we  move from the  general scope of requirements into  something that is specific to Google  cloud and and specifically implementing  secure private and compliant workloads  on Google cloud the first thing that's  important to discuss when talking about  cloud is the shared responsibility model  and specifically the shared  responsibility model in in detail the  the shared responsibility model says  that in contrast to traditional IT  systems where the IT owner is  responsible for maintaining the  infrastructure stack top to bottom with  cloud services that responsibility is  shared between the cloud provider and  the in-house IT team of the organization  and it's beneficial from multiple angles  the shared responsibility model allows  each organization to focus on their  specific projects and core competencies  while Google the cloud provider focuses  on managing the services and the  underlying infrastructure and this can  be from a security standpoint from a  scaling standpoint from a freshness of  the IT stack so to speak making sure  that the machines are up-to-date and  replaced periodically and there are  other benefits from this model as well  it allows organizations to benefit from  Google's world-class security and  privacy teams who are in charge of  maintaining the infrastructure and you  can also take advantage of the cost  savings that stem from Google's ability  to purchase these technologies in bulk  and efficiently manage the data center  environments and delivering the cloud  services so on this diagram you can see  that different levels of services have  different levels of responsibilities  related to security privacy and  compliance and this is generally a  function of how much control the cloud  service user has over the environment so  in an infrastructure as a service or is  service you'll see that there is a fair  amount of responsibility that the  customers IT team still has to manage  but this is also reflected in the level  of control that their IT team has over  the system so if there are requirements  to have a specific guest OS image  you know a specific network topography a  specific level of encryption using  specific key lengths specific encryption  algorithms that level of control comes  with the comparable level of  responsibility to do things  appropriately and therefore you'll see  that the cloud provider Google cannot  manage as much of the system as they can  in other services so when we move kind  of up the stack into more manage  services let's say a platform as a  service offering you can see that the  level of responsibility managed by the  cloud provider increases and this is to  reflect that the cloud provider is now  taking more of the decisions and  responsibilities around scaling security  compliance and providing a managed  service to the user and finally at the  the right-hand side with a  software-as-a-service application the  cloud provider has taken on a majority  of the responsibility in maintaining the  service and then the user only generally  has to worry about what data they put in  the application who they share access  with and so on and so forth so there's a  column missing in this diagram and  that's kind of the traditional IT model  the for cloud services and and that  would be where an organization's  in-house IT team or outsource IT team  was responsible for all of these  activities related to cloud to the  security of the platform and that would  be a fully blue column so to speak so  you can see that even in you know the  most flexible cloud services there's  still a decent amount of responsibility  that the cloud provider is managing so  let's take a look at some specific  examples it related to Google cloud if  we look at a hypothetical shared  responsibility matrix for compute engine  which is our infrastructure as a service  compute platform you'll see that at the  bottom of the stack Google takes full  responsibility for the hardware  underlying the service and we implement  that using our proprietary hardware  stack we also take responsibility for  the boot process the kernel the storage  and encryption at the physical layer as  well as the network  again on the physical site and this is  done through Google's proprietary  technology and hardware stack to make  sure that you know the vulnerabilities  are mitigated attacks can be prevented  and and/or mitigated and that google has  taken responsibility for the security  and compliance of these components  turning focus to the customer  responsibilities they start pretty much  at the anything that is installed on the  virtual machine so the guest OS the  choice of OSS is with the user what data  they put on the machine is up to the  user how they configure the network how  they authenticate users to the system  basically how they scale the virtual  machines up and down depending on  traffic going to the application and  then all the way up to the top of the  stack what web services run on the  virtual machine so there's a there's a  lot of freedom afforded to the users of  compute engine to configure and manage  their applications appropriately but  that also comes with the responsibility  to do it correctly  transitioning to a platform service such  as bigquery you can automatically see  that Google's responsibility has moved  up the stack so to speak Google is now  responsible specifically for maintaining  the operations of the service and what  that means is bigquery scales  automatically for the amount of data  that customers put in the platform so  Google is handling you know adding more  memory to the system adding more storage  adding more compute capacity and  customers do not have to but there is  still customer responsibility here and  the responsibility again goes from which  users have access to these bigquery  instances and bigquery data sets all the  way up to what applications are able to  manipulate data in bigquery going to the  the far end of the spectrum an example  of a SAS platform in Google cloud is  Google Drive and a hypothetical shared  responsibility matrix for this shows  that Google's responsibility moves quite  far up the stack Google is now  responsible for deploying the service in  an in a way that customers can  appropriately access and  but customers again will always retain  some level of responsibility for  choosing what content they put into the  service who is able to access it within  the customer's organization and so on so  if we summarize Google's technology  suite for helping customers with their  shared responsibilities you'll see that  Google has products and services to help  with shared responsibilities in all of  these areas so on the infrastructure  side there are things like shielded VMs  which can help protect the  infrastructure as a service offerings  there are services like data loss  prevention and VPC service controls that  can help these platform-as-a-service  Services manage their data appropriately  and access controls appropriately and  then finally at the top of the stack  even with regards to SAS services there  are endpoint security and Identity and  Access Management solutions to make sure  that access is appropriately controlled  to a SAS application even though the  customer is not responsible for managing  anything below a certain level in that  stack and finally in in regards to the  overview of Google Cloud I mentioned  earlier that there were a number of  government regulations frameworks  industry standards that were relevant to  healthcare and life sciences here is an  overview of Google clouds overall  compliance posture this is not you know  an exhaustive list of everything  customers can do on Google cloud but it  is a representation of frameworks at  Google Cloud 'dearest - some of which  have self attestation some of which have  external third-party attestation and  some of which have specific contractual  indicators as well so at this point I  want to transition focus to setting up  and managing healthcare workloads on  Google cloud and specifically how you  can best manage your shared  responsibilities and add ear to the  various security privacy and compliance  requirements that you need - and one of  the offerings that we have in the space  is called the  health care data protection suite and  the healthcare data protection suite is  an integrated tool for deploying  monitoring and auditing Google cloud  environments and workloads that are  running on Google cloud and by  integrated I mean there is very little  lag between the deployment of the  resources themselves when they start  getting monitored and when they're the  monitoring data is available for  investigation  triage documentation and so on how this  works at a technical level we have  integrated infrastructure as code  deployment templates command line tools  and continuous integration continuous  deployment pipelines to enable easy  verifiable and reproducible deployment  of Google cloud architectures as well as  common healthcare and life sciences  solutions so what this means is we have  some predefined templates for common  healthcare workloads on Google cloud  such as HIPAA aligned deployments or Rd  platforms that account for various  compliance guidance as well as technical  best practices for setting up Google  cloud services and there is a automation  for this deployment there's automation  for validating that everything in these  in Frye's code templates was deployed  appropriately and then there is  continuous monitoring against a set of  defined policies that are defined at the  time of resource creation and having  this suite fully integrated means that  you can define your workload and the  monitoring policy is side-by-side you  can ensure there's no or minimal gaps in  monitoring coverage and start scanning  for policy violations as soon as the  resources are deployed and this is a you  know a little bit more useful than some  other tool suites where the monitoring  deployment and auditing are all separate  steps in some of those other workflows  the to environment gets deployed there  can often be a lag of several hours to  several weeks between when the resources  are deployed and when they begin to be  monitored and then another lag between  the monitoring data is available and  when it's available for triage and  investigation in any sort of UI  interface so if we take a look at how  this works in detail there is a suite of  configuration libraries that we've made  available to our users and what these  allow users to do is create deployment  modules you can have deployment modules  for specific services like bigquery  Google Cloud storage and compute engine  that tell the application how these  services should be configured where they  should be set up what the IAM policy  should look like what the monitoring  rules should look like and then these  are all stored in the form of a  declarative template and once this  template is ready to be deployed we have  integration with terraform for  continuous deployment continuous  integration and automation of the  deployment practices and and what this  allows you to do is you know do your  code reviews on automation code you  don't have to wait for the environment  to be set up to do the validation it  also helps you set up multiple instances  of the exact same infrastructure for  different purposes so you can imagine  potentially some GXP related  requirements where you have to have  identical production development and  test or QA environments and this  automated template framework allows you  to set these up exactly equal to each  other you don't have to rely on making  sure you click the right buttons in the  console or run the right command-line  arguments in the same order every time  diving deeper into these templates the  deployment templates can be written in a  native terraform syntax or in our own  declarative llamó syntax that compiles  in to terraform and different  organizations may have different  preferences for how they use these tools  organizations that have terraform code  already can reuse that or organizations  that don't have existing terraform code  and prefer a declarative llamó syntax  can start there and generate the  terraform output as I mentioned these  deployment templates are modular they  can be  linked together to make larger projects  or organization structures for the  workloads and they also have accounted  for resource specific configuration so  where a specific resource has a  configuration parameter you can account  for that specifically and not have to  set it you know globally after  deployment so this example on the slide  is setting up a project for holding data  and what this is doing is its setting up  a bigquery data set with a data  retention policy to map the specific  privacy or compliance requirement it  sets a data viewer group as well as a  project owner group that has access to  all of the resources within a project  and and and essentially once this  template is deployed it will result in a  bigquery data set with all the  appropriate settings and controls on it  and leaning on from this the resource  deployment feeds directly into the  monitoring policies and the monitoring  policies can be auto-generated based on  the deployment template so where there  is a setting for i.m policy in the  deployment template the tools can infer  that you might want a monitoring policy  to prevent changes to that iam  configuration or the monitoring policies  can also be explicitly specified so if  you have a very specific set of  allowable  contractual requirements with your  customers and any one of those settings  is allowed you can set a monitoring  policy that allows you to set any one of  those configurations but not deviate  from that approved set by default the  scanners for these monitoring controls  run at a default 12 hour interval  however this can be configured to any  interval that you're comfortable with in  your organization the examples of  available monitoring policies are  centered around compliance requirements  specifically the security and privacy  compliance requirements and some  examples are access control lists and  iam policies ensuring that your  on it logging settings are appropriate  making sure that the api's that have  been enabled in your project are  appropriate for what that project will  be doing with data and going you know  further down the list if there are  specific data retention requirements or  data lifecycle requirements those can be  monitored for as well so the example on  the left side is showing how to  explicitly specify monitoring  requirements in the configuration  interface and what this is doing here is  showing that there's two controls that  are being monitored for one is the  policy member domains which you are  allowed to have membership within the  food domain or the bar domain for  example and then the allowed shared VPC  host projects which are declared beneath  and what these monitoring rules will do  is if there's any deviation from you  know the two named policy member domains  or the two VPC shared host projects you  will get an alert in the monitoring  toolset that it claims that there has  been a deviation and finally the last  step in this integrated process is the  auditing and reporting so the the data  protection dashboard here enables users  to visualize track policy violations  captured by these monitoring tools you  can dig into the policy violations view  the the scan results in raw detail as  well as understand long term and short  term policy viol